<section id="intro" class="tab"><h2 class="section-title">Introduction</h2>
<div class="prose">Cyberpunk 2077 is a masterpiece of storytelling in video gaming*, only let down by the extreme ludonarrative dissonance that kicks in once you start act 2 of the game. Ever since I completed my first blind playthrough of the game, on every subsequent playthrough I&#x27;ve devoted part of my time to one extra thng - unlocking the puzzle of an optimal story path. My thinking was there had to be a way to complete all the content, without ever feeling too much like you were completely divorced from the main story and ignoring the ticking clock element of the plot. I regularly see comments along the line of &#x27;why would V bother doing any gigs/side quests?&#x27;, &#x27;why can I take as much time as I want to complete the game after act 2 starts?&#x27;, etc. I&#x27;m being deliberately vague to avoid spoilers, in case any first time players have stumbled across this. Obviously you can play this game however you want, take as much time as you want - maybe the cognitive dissonance between the story and the gameplay doesn&#x27;t bother you as much as it bothers me. But if you&#x27;d like to try and play Cyberpunk in a way that maintains the propulsiveness of the narrative without missing out on any content, then hopefully I can help with this: the ultimate Cyberpunk 2077 story-focused quest order.<br><br>To give credit where it&#x27;s due, I&#x27;m not the first to try something like this, and u/Superkowz put together this guide that I frequently referred to while putting together mine, and their list of timings is excellent and saved me a lot of time. But it doesn&#x27;t guide you through the game on a day to day basis like this does, so is excellent if you&#x27;re just looking for some quick info on when a quest starts rather than this extensive guide.<br><br>I should note now that this is not a guide to 100% completion of the game. I won&#x27;t be telling you what order to complete gigs in, or when to start every side quest. My reasoning for that is that if you&#x27;re a first time player or someone who doesn&#x27;t know the game as well as I do at this point, I can tell you to start The Ballad Of Buck Ravers or Space Oddity in this guide, but quests with those names don&#x27;t appear in your journal until you walk up to the quest marker. So you&#x27;d need to be doing extra research on what quest is what, cross referencing an online map with your in game map, and since none of those quests involve substantial time skips or need to be done at a certain time, I&#x27;ve left them out. Do them as and when you come across them. This guide only refers to quests that you are directly contacted about, whether it&#x27;s by text, conversation, or phone call - quests that I can guarantee everyone will get, with some variance for choices of course. It&#x27;s a framework - the story is the scaffolding that allows for side questing and exploring. <br><br>I&#x27;ve also attempted to make sure nobody is left waiting for V to respond or visit them for longer than a day or two - with some obvious exceptions, of course. Sorry Hanako. I wanted to try and avoid that issue of picking a quest out of the journal and wondering what on earth is going on because you got the call to start it a week ago. Sadly it&#x27;s not always possible, but there are some recommended mods below that will help. The recommended times are aimed at avoiding V arriving at a quest and sitting down for 12 hours solid because you turned up in the morning but starting the quest skips you to the evening. Where time skips are unavoidable, I&#x27;ve lined up quests so that you don&#x27;t notice them.<br><br>There&#x27;s more information on how to read this guide below, along with my personal recommendations for role playing habits and mods to help with the experience I&#x27;m trying to put together. The main guide is as spoiler free as possible - there are quest names, but any notes I&#x27;ve made are as vague as can be, only referring to character names and never events. If you are a first time player, you won&#x27;t find any spoilers in the main guide. However, there is a second version of the guide that includes some personal notes on why I&#x27;ve made certain recommendations, why some quests go where, and those can be a little spoilery so if you don&#x27;t want that, stay with the main one. But I&#x27;ll welcome any discussion on my notes in the comments on the Reddit posts.<br><br>Finally, to answer the big question - if you follow this guide, the events of acts 2 and 3 take place over 35 days. I believe that&#x27;s well within Viktor&#x27;s stated timeframe of &#x27;a few weeks, tops&#x27;. Thanks for checking it out, chooms.<br><br>*opinion is my own, please don&#x27;t yell at me</div>
<h2 class="section-title">How To Read This Guide</h2>
<div class="prose">It should be fairly simple - you have quest names on the left hand side, a recommended time to start it next to it, and any notes on what to do or which dialogue to choose where necessary. Each block is a day of ingame time, and for maximum immersion I highly recommend finding a place for V to sleep after completing a day, unless otherwise noted. Where a quest has a recommended time, if you arrive any time within the time slot (explained on the right hand side) then you either won&#x27;t have to wait, or sitting and waiting won&#x27;t skip too much time. If there&#x27;s no recommended time, then do it whenever - but if a quest without a recommended time appears after one that does, do them in the order as presented.<br><br>Green quests are main quests - if you would prefer to explore side content, including romances, on your own then you should only pay attention to these.<br><br>Orange quests are secondary quests.<br><br>Blue entries are good times to go off and do non-story things - NCPD scanners, fixer gigs, or unnamed side quests visible on your map. Your opportunity to explore at your own pace. I&#x27;ve placed these where you&#x27;ll have a good chunk of time to do them, but really you can do them whenever - if the last quest in a day block finishes in the afternoon, there&#x27;s no reason not to keep grinding scanner hustles or gigs until night falls, for example.</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Role Playing Tips</h2>
<div class="prose">I&#x27;m a big believer that immersion is video gaming&#x27;s great strength as a medium when it comes to storytelling, so the more you can do to feel like you&#x27;re walking in V&#x27;s shoes, the better. With that in mind, here&#x27;s a few things I recommend doing:<br><br>Eat, drink, and sleep. Installing the Dark Future mod will make this a necessicity (more on that later), but even if you aren&#x27;t modding the game, you can still find breaks to walk around the city to find a food stall twice a day, and head home at the end of the day to catch some sleep and take a shower. It helps V feel like a real person, not a freakish automaton.<br><br>Don&#x27;t summon your car at every opportunity. If you do a job, and as part of that job you have to steal a car or are driven somewhere else and have to leave your car behind, then once that job is complete don&#x27;t just summon your car back. Walk to the nearest metro station and travel back to where your car was or all the way back home. I know Night City has self driving cars, and I can believe a car could automatically return to a &#x27;home base&#x27;, but being able to summon it anywhere at the drop of a hat means you often get hung up on driving from A to B instead of living in the moment.<br><br>Don&#x27;t drive directly up to quest markers. Not only will you stop accidentally killing six civillians as they fall into your bumper while you pull up on the sidewalk, you&#x27;ll feel more like a real person living in a real city if you look for a parking spot nearby. The last thing V needs is to get arrested because of a parking ticket, so find a spot.<br><br>Limit when and where you access things like crafting and your wardrobe. Again there are mods that will do this by force, but you can roleplay it too. Tinker with guns in your stash room, only change outfit if you&#x27;re in an apartment or with your car, where V could have a change of clothes in the back seat.<br><br>Call people. If you&#x27;ve not heard from Judy for a while and your V would want to check in with her, try. You&#x27;d be surprised how much dialogue there is in the game that you might not have heard before.<br><br>Don&#x27;t be afraid to tell people you&#x27;re too busy for their quests right now. As far as I&#x27;m aware, any time you have an option to say to someone you don&#x27;t have time for their quest right now, it will not lock you out of the quest. You&#x27;ll just get a journal entry to call them back when you can. This is especially useful for Panam&#x27;s and the Peralez&#x27; follow up quests.</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Recommended Mods</h2>
<div class="prose">There are four mods that I&#x27;d call really important to this guide, if you can mod the game. Those are Live A Little (only use the &#x27;timers&#x27; files), Panam Questline Delays, They Won&#x27;t Go When I Go - Rachel&#x27;s Call Delayed, and Quest Trigger Fixes (compatability file). All of these delay calls about quests (or make sure you get calls, in the case of Spellbound) - the guide was written around those timers, and while you can follow the guide without them you will find yourself leaving some characters hanging for a few days longer. Highly recommended, excellent mods.<br><br>I&#x27;ve already mentioned it, but Dark Future is the most feature complete, smooth integration of survival mechanics I&#x27;ve ever seen in any video game, ever. It&#x27;s fantastic and I can&#x27;t recommend it enough.<br><br>I play with the &#x27;longer days and nights&#x27; version of Game Time Tweaks, which makes each day last longer. You should be able to complete this guide without it, but you will have more time to explore and do side content if the days last longer so this is recommended.</div></section>
<section id="faqs" class="tab"><h2 class="section-title">Spoilers will follow here!</h2>
<div class="prose">Why this order?</div>
<div class="prose">Although Cyberpunk 2077 is an open game and you can do whatever you want in whatever order, over my playthroughs I do think there&#x27;s a &#x27;supposed&#x27; path you&#x27;re meant to take, and that&#x27;s what I&#x27;m recommending. <br><br>My reasoning for this is largely based on Johnny Silverhand. In act 2, V has a lot of dialogue with Johnny that you can&#x27;t control. You can pick certain dialogue options sure, but there&#x27;s a lot of set dialogue that doesn&#x27;t change. No matter what you do during Automatic Love, V and Johnny will always have a slightly hostile relationship. V will always refer to him as a brain parasite, for example. If you do this quest after completing the Ghost Town series of quests, where Johnny admits that he and V might have more in common than they thought after all, that dialogue suddenly becomes jarring. So Johnny and V&#x27;s dialogue became the basis of a lot of the storyline for me. <br><br>There are other things that factor into it - for example, I can&#x27;t recommend starting Phantom Liberty before doing Play It Safe, because it&#x27;s unbelievable to me that Arasaka would go ahead with the parade mere days after the literal NUSA president was shot down over Night City.</div>
<div class="prose">Where&#x27;s Beat On The Brat?</div>
<div class="prose">Although this is a quest that every player gets whether they like it or not, I haven&#x27;t included it in the guide simply because unlike The Beast In Me, most of the quests don&#x27;t have timeskips or take place at certain times of day. You can do them any time you like while side questing. The only exception is the final fight, which has a skip to night, but as I haven&#x27;t included the first quests it didn&#x27;t make sense to randomly put in the final quest. So if you&#x27;re doing it, do it whenever you like.</div>
<div class="prose">Where&#x27;s X quest?</div>
<div class="prose">As explained in the intro, I didn&#x27;t want players to have to keep the Cyberpunk wiki open alongside this guide to cross reference quests that aren&#x27;t named on the map. Most people won&#x27;t know which exclamation mark on the map starts The Ballad Of Buck Ravers, and since none of those quests have a timeskip it wasn&#x27;t necessary to include them specifically in the guide. Do them as part of the side quest blocks.</div>
<div class="prose">I don&#x27;t have a quest you&#x27;ve mentioned yet?</div>
<div class="prose">Cyberpunk&#x27;s quest triggers are a little nebulous, and you can rarely guarantee that someone will call you about something by a certain time. But I&#x27;ve played through this guide twice now, and twice more while I was working on it, and I&#x27;ve never found myself waiting for a call. Make sure that you&#x27;re actually playing the game and not just skipping time waiting for calls. If the worst comes to the worst and you absolutely do not get a call that you should be getting in time to complete it for the guide, then I&#x27;d probably recommend not proceeding with story quests until you&#x27;ve got the call. Do some gigs for a while instead. <br><br>Of course though, make sure that you&#x27;re not waiting for a quest that you&#x27;ll never get. There are some, like Blistering Love and Pyramid Song, that you might never get depending on your actions in previous quests.</div>
<div class="prose">I want to unlock my romance sooner, is this possible?</div>
<div class="prose">By design you&#x27;ll complete your chosen romances in act 3 using this guide. It might be possible to finish a romance earlier - River and Panam in particular, Judy and Kerry are locked behind a lot of quests. So it is possible, but doing so would interrupt the flow of the story in general, and creating a separate early Panam romance guide for example would be a lot of effort so only something I&#x27;d consider if there was a lot of demand.</div>
<div class="prose">Why bother doing this at all?</div>
<div class="prose">Because I truly love this game and wanted to figure out a way to experience it in the most immersive, narratively satisfying way possible. I get a kick out of it, and hopefully someone else will enjoy it too.</div>
<div class="prose">Panam, Judy, River, or Kerry?</div>
<div class="prose">Judy, because I was a teenager at the height of emo in the late 2009&#x27;s so can&#x27;t resist the scene kid, tattoos and dyed hair aesthetic.</div>
<div class="prose">What&#x27;s your favourite Samurai song?</div>
<div class="prose">Never Fade Away and it isn&#x27;t even close.</div></section>
